'''Kii Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|紀伊県}}, ''Kii-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nankai]] Region in the southern highlands of the island of [[Yagikoshu]]. With a population of 443,325 as of 2026 spread across 9,215 km², it is the third-largest prefecture on Yagikoshu by area and one of the most sparsely populated, with a landscape dominated by two volcanic peaks—the active [[Mount Uenohara]] ({{nihongo|上野原岳|Uenohara-dake}}, 1,924 m) and the dormant [[Mount Hayakawa]] ({{nihongo|早川岳|Hayakawa-dake}}, 2,147 m)—and by the [[Yagikoshu-2 Data Corridor]], the island's secondary data centre cluster.

Kii Prefecture is also the heartland of the [[Atsu people]], the indigenous people of southern Yagikoshu, whose {{nihongo|''kotan''|コタン}} village-communities and {{nihongo|''iwor''|イウォル}} sacred hunting grounds are concentrated in the mountainous interior. The prefecture's cultural identity is inseparable from the Atsu presence, and Kii is the centre of the Atsu cultural revival that has gathered momentum since the 1990s.

== History ==

=== The Atsu heartland ===
The Kii highlands have been the centre of Atsu civilisation for millennia. The {{nihongo|Kii Peninsula|紀伊半島|Kii Hantō}}—the southernmost extension of the Yagikoshu mountain spine—sheltered the Atsu {{nihongo|''kotan''|コタン}} from the northward expansion of the Yoroi Sanese, and the region's remote, mountainous terrain preserved Atsu language, religion, and social organisation long after the lowland communities had been assimilated.

The most significant Atsu leader of the pre-imperial period, {{nihongo|Kutkina Samayek|クツキナ・サマエク}}, unified 23 kotan into a military confederation that successfully resisted the [[Mitoyagi Empire]]'s expansion into the Kii highlands for over 60 years. The {{nihongo|Kii Kotan Confederation|紀伊コタン連合|Kii Kotan Rengō}}, though ultimately defeated in 892, established a tradition of Atsu autonomy that persists in the region's identity.

=== The data corridor ===
[[File:Kii Data Centre.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Kii Data Centre, the third-largest data centre complex in Yagikoshu]]
The establishment of the {{nihongo|Yagikoshu-2 Data Corridor|焼木子2データ回廊|Yagikoshu-2 Dēta Kairō}} in the 2020s transformed Kii from one of Okaiken's poorest and most remote prefectures into a node of the national digital infrastructure. The site was chosen for the same reasons as the Obama Data Centre: abundant geothermal energy from the two volcanoes, cool highland temperatures (averaging 12°C, even cooler than Obama), and reliable fibre-optic connections to the [[Toyozawa Data Corridor]] via the [[Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen]] right-of-way.

The {{nihongo|Kii Data Centre|紀伊データセンター|Kii Dēta Sentā}}, opened in 2023, houses approximately 180,000 square metres of server floor space with 360 MW of IT load capacity, making it the third-largest data centre complex in Yagikoshu after the Toyozawa and Obama facilities. The centre is powered entirely by geothermal energy from the {{nihongo|Uenohara Geothermal Plant|上野原地熱発電所|Uenohara Chinetsu Hatsudensho}}, which taps the active volcano's heat.

== Geography ==

Kii Prefecture occupies the southern highlands of Yagikoshu, with no coastline. The prefecture is bounded by [[Mutsu Prefecture]] to the west, [[Uruwa Prefecture]] to the north, and the open Shendan Ocean to the south and east (across mountain ranges). The prefecture's geography is defined by two volcanic peaks and the highland plateau that stretches between them.

[[Mount Uenohara]] (1,924 m) is an active stratovolcano located at the prefecture's western border with Mutsu, near the town of [[Uenohara]]. The volcano's last significant eruption occurred in 2018, when a phreatic explosion produced an ash plume that reached 8,000 metres and forced the temporary evacuation of the Data Centre. The volcano is closely monitored by the [[Kii Volcanological Observatory]], which operates a network of seismometers and tiltmeters on the peak.

[[Mount Hayakawa]] (2,147 m) is a dormant shield volcano at the junction of the towns of [[Hayakawa]], [[Minamino]], [[Koshu]], and [[Nirasaki]]. The volcano has not erupted in over 10,000 years, and its broad, gentle slopes are covered in dense forest and alpine meadows. The {{nihongo|Hayakawa Caldera|早川カルデラ|Hayakawa Karudera}}, a large depression at the summit, contains several crater lakes that are the source of the [[Kii River]].

=== Climate ===
Kii Prefecture has an [[w:Oceanic climate|oceanic climate]] (''Cfb'') at the basin elevation, with cool summers and cold, snowy winters. The highland position means winter temperatures regularly fall below −8°C, and the mountains receive heavy snowfall—over 5 metres annually in the highest areas. Summers are pleasant, with temperatures typically 20–25°C, making the prefecture significantly cooler than the coastal areas of southern Yagikoshu.

