== Economy ==

Kii Prefecture's economy is driven by the Yagikoshu-2 Data Corridor, deep-water fishing via the [[Port of Nankai]], and the Atsu cultural economy. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円16 billion in 2025, with services (data services and tourism) accounting for 45%, industry 30%, and agriculture/fisheries 25%.

=== The Yagikoshu-2 Data Corridor ===
[[File:Kii Data Centre.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Kii Data Centre, the third-largest data centre complex in Yagikoshu]]
The {{nihongo|Kii Data Centre|紀伊データセンター}} is described above. The facility employs approximately 2,800 workers directly and supports an estimated 6,000 additional jobs in construction, maintenance, and support services. The data centre's primary tenants include [[Kizan Systems]] (disaster-recovery backup), [[Satori AI]] (training data processing), and several government agencies that use the facility for secure data storage.

The geothermal power plant that supplies the data centre also provides electricity to the surrounding communities, and the warm water discharge from the cooling systems has been repurposed for {{nihongo|''onsen''|温泉}} baths and greenhouse heating, creating a synergy between the high-technology sector and the traditional mountain economy.

=== Deep-water fishing ===
Although Kii Prefecture is landlocked, the {{nihongo|Port of Nankai|南海港|Nankai-kō}}—located on the Shendan Ocean coast at the prefecture's southeastern tip—provides access to some of the richest deep-water fishing grounds in southern Yagikoshu. The Nankai fleet operates approximately 180 vessels, landing approximately 55,000 tonnes of seafood annually, primarily {{nihongo|Patagonian toothfish|メロ|mero}}, {{nihongo|orange roughy|金眼鯛|kinmedai}}, and deep-sea shrimp.

The {{nihongo|Nankai Fish Market|南海魚市場|Nankai Gyoshijō}} is the primary wholesale market for deep-water species in southern Yagikoshu, and the port's cold-storage facilities allow fresh fish to be transported by rail to the Yagikoshu mainland within hours of landing.

=== Atsu cultural economy ===
[[File:Kii Atsu Cultural Centre.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Kii Atsu Cultural Centre, attracting approximately 400,000 visitors annually]]
The Atsu cultural revival has become an increasingly important economic asset for Kii Prefecture. The {{nihongo|Kii Atsu Cultural Centre|紀伊阿津文化センター|Kii Atsu Bunka Sentā}}, opened in 2008, houses a museum, language school, and performance space, and attracts approximately 400,000 visitors annually. The {{nihongo|Atsu Craft Cooperative|阿津工芸協同組合|Atsu Kōgei Kyōdō Kumiai}} produces traditional Atsu textiles, woodcarvings, and ceremonial objects that are sold as high-end cultural products, generating approximately 円3.2 billion in annual revenue.

The {{nihongo|Kii Kotan Trail|紀伊コタン道|Kii Kotan-michi}}—a 120 km hiking route that connects the historic Atsu village sites—has become one of Okaiken's most popular trekking destinations, attracting approximately 180,000 hikers annually.
Kii anchors the southern tip of Yagikoshu, combining deep-sea oceanographic research with the digital infrastructure of the Yagikoshu-2 Data Corridor. The Port of Nankai hosts abyssal exploration vessels and subsea cabling ships that maintain oceanic sensor arrays along the Shendan Trench. In the forested interior, master charcoal burners selectively prune holm oak to fuel Kishu binchotan kilns, producing exceptionally dense, pure-carbon white charcoal that rings like iron when struck and provides smoke-free, intense radiant heat for elite culinary traditions.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen]] connects [[Kii Station (Okaiken)|Kii Station]] to [[Toyozawa Central Station]] in approximately 150 minutes. The line is currently being extended northward toward [[Sakata]] and [[Aomori]], with projected completion in 2035.

=== Air ===
[[Kii Airport]] (IATA: KII) operates domestic flights to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]].

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Kii's cuisine is shaped by the mountains and the Atsu heritage. The prefecture's signature dish is {{nihongo|''ohaw''|オハウ}}—a traditional Atsu soup made with salmon, konbu, and wild vegetables, flavoured with animal fat and served at communal gatherings. Other specialities include {{nihongo|''Kii houtou''|紀伊ほうとう}}—a hearty mountain stew of flat wheat noodles and pumpkin, and {{nihongo|''sayo-nakh''|サヨナフ}}—Atsu-style grilled venison with wild herbs.

=== Festivals ===
* {{nihongo|'''Imekanu Festival'''|イメカヌ祭り|Imekanu Matsuri}} — Annual Atsu cultural festival (September), the largest indigenous cultural event in Okaiken
* {{nihongo|'''Kii Data Festival'''|紀伊データ祭り|Kii Dēta Matsuri}} — Annual technology and data centre open day (March)
* {{nihongo|'''Hayakawa Snow Festival'''|早川雪祭り|Hayakawa Yuki Matsuri}} — Winter snow and ice festival (August)

